 rue is commonly dismissed as  being just a junkie in all fairness last  summer there were like three weeks where  i thought you were dead or like when  cassie's potential hookup cruelly tells  her that any guy who says he's  interested in you beyond just you  is full of here both women are being  seen as simple abstractions based on one  aspect of their being their drug use or  their sexuality true empathy in contrast  requires seeing people as more than the  sum of their parts and attempting to  connect to their subjectivity as writer  elie wiesel put it we must not see any  person as an abstraction instead we must  see in every person a universe with its  own secrets with its own treasures with  its own sources of anguish and with some  measure of triumph perhaps  unsurprisingly due to their ability to  viscerally render characters in her  worlds film and later television have  long been considered useful mechanisms  for eliciting empathy as film critic  roger ebert once said for me the movies  are like a machine that generates  empathy now there's one good reason any  successful tv show needs to elicit  empathy people are more likely to keep  coming back week after week if they  actually give a [ __ ] about the  characters on screen but euphoria from  our view is different here empathy isn't  merely a mechanism to get people to  stick around it's the entire point of  the show euphoria most literally spells  this out when rue's narcotics anonymous  sponsor talks about the way society  reduces people struggling with addiction  to abstractions we're too busy  running around judging everybody's  intentions and motivations if we have  some insight into the human soul you  know you did this so that must mean  you're that just give me a break but  more subtly the intention of empathy is  telegraphed throughout the show's visual  and narrative devices take the episodes  which feature rue's voice over narration  she just knew that she had something  special  something in