ose-up one of actress mary pickford's  face this suddenly gave us access to far  more information about her emotional  condition it's perhaps no wonder then  that mary pickford became hollywood's  true first celebrity while actors and  actresses had previously gone uncredited  the rise of close-ups made audiences  start to relate to the performers as  people and thus  love them as much as you love tom daya  euphoria's close-ups are often slow in  searching typically featuring a face or  part of a face over a blurred out  background this gives viewers nothing  but that actor to engage with putting us  as close to understanding what the  character feels as visually possible but  when it's not making you want to barf in  the hot tub with cassie euphoria is also  using the kinds of high octane camera  movements you're more accustomed to  seeing in action films while being  highly cinematic such movements also  pump up our emotions the camera might  dive into a pool to express the  exuberance of a drunken house party or  it might slowly dolly back to emphasize  just how alone a character feels it  might defy gravity to help you get a  sense of what drugs feel like or of  course it might run uninterrupted  through a food cart around the grounds  of a carnival and ultimately up to a  ferris wheel showing the personal  intrigues and heartaches of each  character along the way the sequence in  particular reinforces one of the show's  key themes the characters are all in the  same place physically sure but they're  each in a complicated emotional universe  of their own such is the unadulterated  rollercoaster of teenage hormones and  some of those rollercoaster rides are  relentlessly long it's pretty well  accepted that lengthy shots can be super  effective in building tension more so  than quick cutaways that's why the  lingering shot and say every slasher  movie when you're waiting for the killer  to strike is so much scarier than the  actual slashing itself our instinct is  to look away in search of some emotional  relief by lingering almost unbearably  long in many of its shots euphoria  doesn't give us that relief instead like  a slasher film it makes us sit in the  moment as the tension builds but instead  of a scary guy with a knife the thing  we're confronting is the high intensity  emotions being felt by the characters  euphoria is also known for its endless  tracking chats which give it a feeling  of constant attention and surveillance  accumulating tension with each moment  which isn't all that different from how  it feels to be a teenager painfully  self-aware worried that everyone's  watching and judging you what's more the  colors are just as vivid as the camera  movements a bike ride to meet a crush  takes on a dreamy yellow palette that  evokes that ineffable sense of being in  a fairy tale the first time you fall in  love pinkish hues express the  overwhelming joy of an intoxicated night  out with friends and of course a dark  blue palette emphasizes the utter depths  of roo's depression often times in  modern cinema and television shaky  handheld camera movements are used to  evoke a point of view and literally put  you in the character's shoes in order to  better understand their experience but  euphoria's highly stylized choices do  something different they evoke the  character's outsized feelings about  their experiences as the creator sam  levinson explained he eschewed cinematic  realism and instead strove to make each  scene an interpretation of reality or a  representation of an emotional reality  each shot contributes to an emotional  sense of what the character is feeling  rather than the actual movements of  their everyday life critics sometimes  wonder why euphoria has to take place in  high school asking why the characters  couldn't just be 20 somethings with  fully developed frontal cortexes a  choice which would probably render the  show less controversial but the  emotional realism is key to  understanding why euphoria only makes  sense when set in high school when  you're a teenager all your emotions are  heightened to the nth degree love does  feel like being subsumed in a bath of  pink light having a drink does make the  world feel like it flipped over while we  might not have had the same experiences  as the way two experienced euphoria  teens have we can certainly relate to  the outsized feelings that we're  cinematically persuaded to share with  them but we still haven't fully  explained why empathy is so important to  understanding euphoria and its  perpetually troubled teens to piece that  together we need to look back to rifkin  who argues that we are in an era of  dramaturgical consciousness this means  we take a more theatrical view of life  and our identities all the world's a  stage in a society that's ardently  preoccupied with social media and  subsumed by surveillance it's no  surprise that teens glued to their  phones construct their lives with the  attention to detail of both audience and  director and those audiences and the  performances they expect to see  change with every interaction that means  we essentially construct and reconstruct  ourselves from interaction to  interaction like cal camwork brings out  a different side of cat as she plays the  role men seem to want now role playing  therapy where you act out different  personas to better understand your  psychological hang-ups was common  amongst the baby boomers in their young  adulthood but rifkin says role-playing  is no longer a therapeutic technique but  rather a form of consciousness like how  you play someone different on weird  twitter than in an email to your mom in  our increasingly digital reality we are  all constantly reconstructing and  re-understanding ourselves in every  interaction creating dispersed  identities that are more complex and  nuanced than the fixed understanding  people used to have about human nature  we see this in the narrative  construction of euphoria 